From c45fa281a206884935d7878dce17a5bf3cd5b75f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cedric Robert <cedric.robert@f2i-consulting.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:25:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Sized reads: every remaining one-dataset read passes the
 size of its buffer

The representations and properties left on the pointer forms by patch 3
move to the sized reads: 165 sites in 38 files (point sets, polylines,
polyline sets, triangulated sets, unstructured grids, 2D grids, deviation
surveys, 2.2 wellbore trajectories, explicit and parametric IJK grids,
blocked wellbores, streamlines, sub-representations, sealed and surface
frameworks, grid connection sets, the abstract grid (parent windows,
regrids, stratigraphic and fluid phase units, truncation), seismic
coordinates and traces, seismic wellbore frames, points and realization
indices).

Each site hands the proxy COMMON_NS::Span(buffer, count) where the count
is the one the XML gives and the caller already used to size its buffer
(node, cell, pillar, line, interval, patch, contact counts; the Count
dimensions of an EML 2.3 external data array part, through the new
AbstractObject::getCountOfDataArrayPart). Where the XML does not carry the
count (the elements of a jagged array, regrid child cell weights, the
truncation index lists, the columns of split coordinate lines), the count
of the array itself is used (getCountOfArray or getElementCount): no more
round trips than before on a store, and the buffer can no longer be
overrun.

The hyperslab forms, which already carry their sizes, are untouched. After
this patch no read inside fesapi uses the pointer forms, which can be
deprecated.

Found by the extent check of the sized reads: the RESQML 2.2 blocked
wellbore writer announced in its XML parts NodeCount grid indices,
CellCount cell indices and CellCount*2 local face pairs while writing one
value (two for the pairs) per interval, NodeCount - 1 of them; the parts
now announce what is written, and the reader sizes its buffers by the
interval count. The 2.0.1 reader already worked per interval.
